<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonidas I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/480-bce">480 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Leonidas of Sparta · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonidas I was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> of the Agiad line who commanded the Greek force defending the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> against the Persian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> in 480 BCE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> outflanked the pass, he sent most of the allies away and fell fighting with his 300 Spartans and their remaining allies. His stand became the classic example of Spartan courage and sacrifice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonidas-i.jpg" alt="Leonidas I" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: de:Benutzer:Ticinese · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmed_Hoplite_Sparta.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 480 BCE <b>Leonidas I</b> held the pass of Thermopylae with a small Greek force including 300 Spartiates until he was outflanked and killed, and in 479 BCE the regent Pausanias commanded the Greek army that destroyed the Persian land forces at the Battle of Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Gorgo, daughter of Cleomenes I and wife of <b>Leonidas I</b>, was remembered for her advice, and Cynisca, sister of Agesilaus II, became the first woman to win an Olympic chariot race, in 396 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought with <b>Leonidas</b> at Thermopylae in 480 BCE, then were forced to join the Persians, and later usually sided with Thebes and Sparta against Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/opuntian-locris">Opuntian Locris</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Thermopylae, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-490" aria-label="Sparta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5112 828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leonidas I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5562 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5562 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-490">What was here in 490 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
